[Lem basicwalk of arXiv:2411.12149, 'Airy limit for $\beta$-additions through Dunkl operators'] For $H\in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0},\ L\in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 100}$, sample the walk $W(t)$ from $\mathcal{\tilde{W}}(0,\cdot,L;N)$ and  $\mathcal{\tilde{W}}(\cdot, 0,L;N)$ respectively.
    Then there exist constants $C>0$, $c>0$ uniform in $L,N$, such that for all $h\ge 0$,
    \begin{align*}
       \mathbb{P}[W\ge 0, W(L)=\lfloor h\sqrt{L}\rfloor]\le \frac{C}{L}\quad &\text{if}\ \ W(t)\in \tilde{\mathcal{W}}(0,\cdot,L;N),\\
        \mathbb{P}[W\ge 0, W(0)=\lfloor h\sqrt{L}\rfloor]\le \frac{C}{L}\exp(-ch^{2})\quad &\text{if}\ \ W(t)\in \tilde{\mathcal{W}}(\cdot ,0,L;N).
    \end{align*}

[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2605.26439, '2605.26439'] For the equation \eqref{H equation}, assume there exist constants $C,\alpha>0$ and $s\in[0,1)$ such that $f\in H^{-s}(0,1)$ and
    \begin{equation}
        |\langle f,e_n\rangle|\geq C e^{-\alpha \pi^2n^2}\quad \forall\, n\in\N^+.
    \end{equation}
    Then the Markov semigroup $\{P_t\}_{t\geq 0}$ on $L^2(0,1)$ associated with equation \eqref{H equation} is strong Feller at any time $T>\alpha$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 20 (Affine reconstruction from reduced coordinates) of arXiv:2604.10792, 'Variable-Length Markov Chains on Finite Quivers: Boundary-Window Identifiability, Exact De'] Fix a visible depth $\ell$ and let $\mathcal A_\ell$ be the affine subspace of arrays satisfying the forced-zero and row-sum constraints. If $L_\ell$ is a reduced coordinate chart, then $L_\ell(\mathcal A_\ell)$ is an affine subspace of $\RR^{N_\ell}$ and the inverse map
\begin{equation*}
(L_\ell|_{\mathcal A_\ell})^{-1}:L_\ell(\mathcal A_\ell)\to\mathcal A_\ell
\end{equation*}
is affine.
In particular, every full transition coordinate on $\mathcal A_\ell$ is an affine function of the reduced coordinates.

[Lemma exponential-estimate-lemma3 of arXiv:2411.04500, '\LARGE Probabilistic Approaches to The Energy Equality in Forced Surface Quasi-Geostrophic'] Let $\varepsilon,\delta(\varepsilon) $ and $\theta_{\varepsilon,\delta(\varepsilon)}$ satisfy the conditions in Lemma (exponential-estimate-lemma2 of the paper). Fix $\sigma\in(0,1/2)$ and $l>3+2\beta-\alpha$. Then there exists $\eta_0>0$ such that for every  $\eta\in[0,\eta_0]$, under the scaling regime (equation scaling-delta of the paper), we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\varepsilon\log\mathbb{E}\Big(\exp\Big\{\frac{\eta}{\varepsilon}\|\theta_{\varepsilon,\delta(\varepsilon)}\|_{C^{\sigma}([0,T];H^{-l}(\mathbb{T}^2))}^2\Big\}\Big)\leqslant C(\eta,T), 
	\end{equation*}
	and
	\begin{equation*}
		\limsup_{R\rightarrow\infty}\sup_{\varepsilon\in(0,1)}\varepsilon\log\mathbb{P}\Big(\|\theta_{\varepsilon,\delta(\varepsilon)}\|_{C^{\sigma}([0,T];H^{-l}(\mathbb{T}^2))}>R\Big)=-\infty.  
	\end{equation*}

