where we write again $W_j = \log (X_{j+1}/Y_j)$, and $\wtl W_j=W_j-\ev[W_j]$.
By the moment estimates \eqref{eq:Wj_moments} for $W_j$, we have $\sum_{j=k_1}^{k_2-1}(\ev[W_j]+\log \gamma_j)=O(\frac{a}{n-k_2})$, with an absolute constant in the $O(\cdot)$ term. Since $n-k\ge \lfloor a \mathfrak{f}(a) \rfloor$, this term can be further bounded by a constant multiple of $\mathfrak{f}(a)^{-1}$, which goes to $0$ as $n\to \infty$.  On the good event ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:a4623c9390855f1b9a9d6fc4af76f10332f1813170522dc3a0521d652ae99dff","revision_digest":"sha256:d533b38a780905c290c88a0ed21c373f78d9ee3b18fa653cd75a7c2feb34190a","source_id":"arxiv:2607.08536","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-07","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

