[Lemma lemma_moments_time of arXiv:2411.16452, 'Interface scaling limit for the critical planar Ising model perturbed by a magnetic field'] Let $t \in (0,\infty)$. Almost surely, for any $k \geq 1$,
\begin{equation} 
    \lim_{m \to \infty} \sum_{(x_1, \dots, x_k) \in \Omega_{m,t}^k}C_{\sigma}^{-k}\delta_m^{\frac{15k}{8}}\EEpmtime{m}{t}[\sigma_{x_1}\dots \sigma_{x_k}] = \int_{\Omega_t^k} f_t^{(\pm, k)}(x_1,\dots, x_k) \prod_{j=1}^{k}dx_j
\end{equation}
where the functions $f_t^{(\pm, k)}:=f_{\Omega_t}^{(\pm, k)}$ are as in Theorem (theorem_spin_correlations of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm1 of arXiv:2510.17257, 'Rigidity of One-Dimensional Point Processes via Optimal Transport'] In dimension $d=1$, let $\mathsf{P}\in\mathscr{P}_s(\Gamma)$ be the law of a stationary point process. Under the assumption 
	\eq{\mathsf{Wass}_1(\mathsf{P},\mathsf{P}^{\mathbb{Z}})<+\infty,} 
	the point process $\mathsf{P}$ is Number-Rigid and has a Cyclic-Factor. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2607.09575, 'Probabilistic estimates for a system of\\noisy integrate-and-fire neurons'] For any $s,t \in [0,T]$, we may find a $C > 0$, independent of $s$, $t$ and $N$, such that 
	\begin{equation}
		\E\left[\nnnorm{F_t^{D,N} - F_s^{D,N}}\right] \le C\nnnorm{t- s}.
	\end{equation}

