body:
[Proposition 9 of arXiv:2608.20252, '2608.20252'] A family $Q^N$ of probability measures on $\mathcal{D}([0,T], \Omega)$ is tight exactly when
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] For each $t\in [0,T]$, the distributions of $\pi_t$ under $Q^N$ are tight.
\item[(2)] For every $\epsilon>0$, $\lim_{\gamma\downarrow 0}\lim_{N\uparrow\infty} Q^N(\pi: w'_\pi(\gamma)>\epsilon) = 0$.
\end{itemize}
Moreover, a sufficient condition for $(2)$ is
\begin{itemize}
\item[(2')] In condition $(2)$, replace $w'_\pi$ with $w_\pi$.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem geo of arXiv:2604.14122, 'The scaling limit of random walk and\\ the intrinsic metric on planar critical percolation'] Let $d=d^{{\mathrm {geo}}}$. For each $V\in\mathfrak{C}$, the metric $(\mathcal{X}\cap\overline{V},\mathfrak{d}_{V})$ is a geodesic metric space, i.e., for all $x,y\in\mathcal{X}\cap\overline{V}$,
\begin{equation}
    \mathfrak{d}_V(x,y)=\inf_{\gamma\in P(x,y;\overline{V})}L_{\mathfrak{d}_V}(\gamma),
\end{equation}
and the infimum above is attained by some path $\gamma\in  P(x,y;\overline{V})$.

[Definition def:capacity-R3 of arXiv:2411.13505, 'Capacity of loop-erased random walk'] Let $A\subset\mathbb{R}^3$. Let $M$ be a standard Brownian motion and denote by $\mathbb{P}^M_y$ the law of $M$ with $M(0)=y$. The \emph{capacity} of $A$ is defined\footnote{As in the discrete case, there are several different equivalent definitions of capacity, but for our purposes we only need the one stated here. We refer to [morters2010brownian] for more background.} as
\begin{equation}
    \Ca_{\mathbb{R}^3}(A):=\lim_{\|y\|\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\mathbb{P}_y^M(M[0,\infty)\cap A\neq\varnothing)}{G_{\mathbb{R}^3}(0,y)},
\end{equation}
with $G_{\mathbb{R}^3}$ the Green's function of Brownian motion on $\mathbb{R}^3$.

[Lemma lem:recursion-fnk of arXiv:2411.03065, 'Growing conditioned BGW trees with\\ log-concave offspring distributions'] The array $(\PFforests{{\mathbf{w}}}{n,k})_{n,k\geq 1}$ satisfies the recursion
	\begin{align}
	\PFforests{{\mathbf{w}}}{n,k}
		=\sum_{i\geq 0}w_i \,\PFforests{{\mathbf{w}}}{n-1,k+i-1},
	\qquad n\geq 2,\qquad k\geq 1,
	\end{align}
	where we make the convention that $\PFforests{{\mathbf{w}}}{n,0}=0$ for all $n\geq 1$.

