body:
[Prop eq:main_eq of arXiv:2606.06187, 'How to Study Reflected Brownian Motion in a Quadrant via Kernel Functional Equations?\\ \m'] For $x,y,u\in\mathbb{C}$ such that $\Re x<0$, $\Re y<0$, and $\Re u<0$, we have
\begin{equation}

    -\bigl(K(x,y)+u\bigr)\widehat{p}(u,x,y)
    = k_1(x,y) \widehat{p}_1(u,y)
    + k_2(x,y) \widehat{p}_2(u,x)
    + e^{\langle (x,y),z_0\rangle},
\end{equation}
where $K,k_1,k_2$ are defined by
\begin{equation}

    \left\{
    \begin{array}{rcl}
    K(x,y) & = & \frac{1}{2}(x^2+y^2+2\rho xy)+\mu_1x+\mu_2y,\\
    k_1(x,y) & = & x+r_1y,\\
    k_2(x,y) & = & r_2x+y.
    \end{array}
    \right.
\end{equation}

[Corollary 2 (Upper bound for H\"older functions) of arXiv:2608.31157, 'Sharp Approximation Rates for Neural Networks with Affine Latent Parameterizations'] Let $d,M,P\in\N^+$ satisfy the assumptions of
Theorem~\ref{thm:upper}, and let $(\Phi,\calA)$ be the single pair supplied
by that theorem.  Let $0<\alpha\le1$ and $\lambda\ge0$.  If
$f\in C([0,1]^d)$ satisfies
$\lvert f(\bmx)-f(\bmy)\rvert
\le\lambda\lVert\bmx-\bmy\rVert_2^\alpha$
for all $\bmx,\bmy\in[0,1]^d$, then
\begin{equation*}
 \inf_{\bmtheta\in\calA(\R^M)}
 \left\lVert f-\Phi_{\bmtheta}\right\rVert_{L^\infty([0,1]^d)}
 \le
 \overline{C}_{\alpha,d}\lambda
 [P\min\{M,P\}]^{-\alpha/d},
\end{equation*}
where $\overline{C}_{\alpha,d}:=(d+2)C_d^\alpha$.  In particular,
\begin{equation}

 \calE_{\alpha,d}(M,P)
 \le
 \overline{C}_{\alpha,d}
 [P\min\{M,P\}]^{-\alpha/d}.
\end{equation}
The same approximants also satisfy this bound in $L^p([0,1]^d)$ for every
$1\le p<\infty$.

[Proposition volterra-resolvent of arXiv:2609.00425, 'Individual and Uniform Eventual Positivity of Self-Adjoint Semigroups'] Let $k$ and $\rho$ be as in Lemma~(lemma:convolution of the paper). For each $f \in L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,\infty))$, the Volterra equation
    \begin{align*}
        y = f + k \ast y
    \end{align*}
    has a unique solution $y \in L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,\infty))$, which is given by
    \begin{align*}
        y = f + \rho \ast f.
    \end{align*}

