[Prop prop_free_boundary_equations of arXiv:2606.23648, '\textsc{Optimal Stopping for a Diffusion with Unobserved Bernoulli Drift}'] Recall the notation of (equation def_phi_2d of the paper), and let \(x\in\mathcal I\). If \(a(x)>-x\), then the free-boundaries $a(\cdot), b(\cdot)$ satisfy
    \begin{equation}
        \Phi(x,a(x))=\Phi(x,b(x)),
        \qquad
        \int_{a(x)}^{b(x)}
            \big(\Phi(x,z)-\Phi(x,a(x))\big)\,dG(z)=0.
    \end{equation}
    If \(a(x)=-x\), then the free-boundary $b(\cdot)$ satisfies
    \begin{equation}
        \int_{-x}^{b(x)}
            \big(\Phi(x,z)-\Phi(x,b(x))\big)\,dG(z)=0.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:shol=3term of arXiv:2509.08928, 'The near critical random bond Ising model via embedding deformation'] Let $\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{S}_\mathcal{X}$ be a proper $s$-embedding and $F$ an $s$-holomorphic on $\diamondsuit(G)$. Given $z\in\diamondsuit(G)$, a corner \mbox{$c\in\Upsilon^\times(G)$} belonging to the quad $z$, one can define the spinor $X$ at $c \in \Upsilon^\times $ by the formula 
\begin{align}
X(c)\ &:=\ |\mathcal{S}(v^\bullet(c))-\mathcal{S}(v^\circ(c))|^{\frac{1}{2}}\cdot\operatorname{Re}[\overline{\eta}_c F(z)] \notag\\
 &=\ \operatorname{Re}[\overline{\varsigma}\mathcal{X}(c)\cdot F(z)]\ =\ \overline{\varsigma}\mathcal{X}(c)\cdot \mathrm{Proj}[F(z);\eta_c\mathbb{R}].

\end{align}
The map $c\mapsto X(c) $ satisfies all three terms identities  (equation eq:3-terms of the paper) around the quad $z$. Conversely given $X:\Upsilon^\times(G)\to\mathbb{R}$ a real valued solution to (equation eq:3-terms of the paper), there exists a unique s-holomorphic function $F$ on $\diamondsuit(G)$ such that the identity (equation eq:X-from-F of the paper) holds. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Conj of arXiv:2605.04860, 'Branching Brownian motion with rank-based selection and reaction-diffusion equations'] Let $\psi$ be a selection function such that $\psi(x)\geq \delta$ for all $x\in [0,1]$ and $\psi(x)=\delta$ for all $x\in [1-p,1]$ for some $p \in (0,1)$. Then the $(\psi,1,N)$-BBM has asymptotic velocity:
    $$v^\psi_N=\sqrt{2(1-\delta)}-\frac{c}{(\log N)^2} + o\left(\frac{1}{(\log N)^2}\right)$$
    for some positive constant $c$. (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

