body:
[Lemma lem-correspondence of arXiv:2411.12623, 'Random signed measures'] There exists a one-to-one almost sure correspondence between a purely atomic random signed measures $\xi$ and a marked signed point process on $\mathcal{S}$, $N_\xi$ say, satisfying
	\begin{equation}
		\int_{A\times \mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}} |s|N_{\xi}(ds\times dx)<\infty\quad\textnormal{a.s.}
	\end{equation}
	for every $A\in\hat{\mathcal{S}}$; the correspondence is the following: almost surely we have that for every $A\in\hat{\mathcal{S}}$ and every $B\in \mathcal{B} (\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\})$ such that $0\notin\bar{B}$
	\begin{equation}
		\xi(A)=	\int_{A\times \mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}} sN_{\xi}(ds\times dx)
	\end{equation}
	and
	\begin{equation}
		N_{\xi}(A\times B)=\lim\limits_{\varepsilon\to 0}\lim\limits_{n\to\infty}\sum_{j}\delta_{\xi(A_{nj})}(B_{\varepsilon}),\quad\text{for $B$ open,}
	\end{equation}
	\begin{equation}
		N_{\xi}(A\times B)=\lim\limits_{\varepsilon\to 0}\lim\limits_{n\to\infty}\sum_{j}\delta_{\xi(A_{nj})}(B^{\varepsilon}),\quad\text{for $B$ closed,}
	\end{equation}
	where $(A_{nj})$ is a dissection system of measurable subsets of $A$. Moreover,  almost surely we have that for every $A\in\hat{\mathcal{S}}$ 
	\begin{equation*}
		\xi_+(A)=	\int_{A\times (0,\infty)} sN_{\xi}(ds\times dx),\quad\text{and}\quad 	\xi_-(A)=	-\int_{A\times (-\infty,0)} sN_{\xi}(ds\times dx)
	\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

Let N take values in {0, ..., m}, 1 <= k <= m and Y = (N - k + 1)_+. Put A_k = sum_{j=k}^m P(N >= j) and B_k = sum_{j=k}^m (2j - 2k + 1) P(N >= j). Then A_k = E[Y] and B_k = E[Y^2], and if A_k > 0, P(N >= k) >= A_k^2/B_k. If moreover mu = E[N] > k - 1, then A_k^2/B_k >= (mu - k + 1)^2/E[N^2], the bound from applying the second-moment threshold argument to N itself; the two agree for k = 1, and for k >= 2 the first is strictly larger.
N takes values in {0, ..., m}
1 <= k <= m

[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2604.01336, 'Euler scheme for stochastic functional differential equations driven by fractional Brownia'] Under Hypotheses \ref{H1} and \ref{H2}, equation \eqref{eq1} has a unique solution $X$ in $C^\lambda_{\xi, 0, T}(\mathbb{R})$.
		Furthermore, for all $(s, t)\in \mathcal S_{2}([0,T])$,
		\begin{equation}

			\delta X({s, t})=f(\overline{X}_s)\delta B^H(s, t) + R^{X}(s,t),
		\end{equation}
		where $R^X$ satisfies
		\begin{equation}

			|R^{X}(s,t)|\leq C_X |t-s|^{2\lambda},\quad
			\text{for some positive constant}\  C_X.
		\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2607.11608, '2607.11608'] Let $k\in \N$. Then, we have 
\begin{align*}
\|X\|_{L^{p}(\Omega)}\le (p-1)^{\frac{k}{2}}\|X\|_{L^{2}(\Omega)},
\end{align*}
for any $p\ge 2$ and $X\in \mathcal{H}_{\le k}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

