[Theorem Gonzalez and Qing \cite{GonzQing} of arXiv:2609.00324, 'Quantitative Stability for Fractional Yamabe minimizers'] Let $\gamma \in (0,1)$ and $a= 1-2\gamma$. There exists a constant $\bar{S} (n,\gamma)$ such that, for every $U\in \dot{W}^{1,2}(\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n+1}, y^a )$ with trace $TU =w$, the following inequality holds
    \begin{align*}
        \|w \|_{L^{2^{*}}(\mathbb{R}^n )}^2 \leq \bar{S}(n,\gamma) \int_{\mathbb{R}_{+}^{n+1}} y^a |\nabla U|^2 dxdy \, ,
    \end{align*}
    where $\bar{S}(n,\gamma) = d_\gamma^* S(n,\gamma)$. Equality holds if and only if
    \begin{align*}
        w(x) = c \left(\frac{\mu}{\mu^2 + |x-x_0 |^2}\right)^{\frac{n-2\gamma}{2}} \quad x \in \mathbb{R}^n,
    \end{align*}
    for $c \in \mathbb{R}$, $\mu >0$ and $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}^n$, and $U$ is the Poisson extension of $w$ given by the convolution with the Poisson kernel. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 6 of arXiv:2608.29554, 'Limiting Behavior of a Class of Hermitian Yang--Mills Metrics, II: Exponential Approximati'] There exists a constant $C>1$, independent of $\epsilon$, such that for
all sufficiently small $\epsilon>0$,
\begin{equation}
C^{-1}\epsilon H_*
\leq H_{0,\epsilon}
\leq C\epsilon^{-1}H_*.

\end{equation}
In particular, for any $\alpha\in A^1(\End(V))$,
\begin{equation}
\|\alpha\|_{L^2(H_*,\omega)}^2
\leq
C\epsilon^{-3}
\|\alpha\|_{L^2(H_{0,\epsilon},\omega_\epsilon)}^2.

\end{equation}

