[Lemma lem:exp-lower of arXiv:2608.20537, 'Persistent anisotropy in multi-dimensional\\ branching Brownian motion'] There exists a constant $\mathring{c} \in (0, 1]$ such that for each $\mathfrak{t} \leq -1$, there exists a function $\mathring{u}(t,x; \mathfrak{t}) \geq 0$ satisfying the following properties:
  \begin{enumerate}[label = \textnormal{(\roman*)},itemsep = 2pt]
  \item $\mathring{u} = 0$ where $t \ll -1$ or $x \geq \left |t \right|$;

  \item

    $\mathring{u}$ is a subsolution of (equation eq:ancient of the paper);

  \item $\partial_x \mathring{u} \leq 0$; and

  \item for all $x \in [0, \abs{\mathfrak{t}}/2]$,
    \begin{equation}

      \mathring{u}(\mathfrak{t},x; \mathfrak{t}) \geq \mathring{c} \mathrm{e}^{-x}.
    \end{equation}
  \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

