[Cor lem:simplyconnleaves of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a cooriented $C^0$-foliation on $M$ transverse to $\partial M$ (if nonempty). If $\Lambda$ is a minimal set of $\mathcal{F}$ consisting of simply connected leaves, then one of the cases in the following table holds:

\begin{center}
\begin{tblr}{
  colspec = {|Q[c,m]|Q[l,m,wd=6cm]|Q[l,m,wd=6.5cm]|},
  row{1} = {bg=gray!20},
  column{1} = {bg=gray!20},
  vlines,
  hlines,
}
  & \centering $\Lambda$ is a compact leaf& \centering $\Lambda$ is not a compact leaf \\
  $\partial M = \varnothing$ & $\Lambda \cong S^2$, and $\mathcal{F}$ is conjugate to the standard foliation by spheres on $S^1 \times S^2$. & $\Lambda$ is a $\mathbb{T}^3$-type planar minimal set, and $\mathcal{F}$ is semi-conjugate to an irrational foliation on $\mathbb{T}^3$.\\
  $\partial M \neq \varnothing$ & $\Lambda \cong D^2$, and $\mathcal{F}$ is conjugate to the standard foliation by disks on $S^1 \times D^2$. & $\Lambda$ is a $\mathbb{T}^2$-type planar minimal set, and $\mathcal{F}$ is semi-conjugate to an irrational foliation on $\mathbb{T}^2 \times I$.\\
\end{tblr}
\end{center}

[Quest of arXiv:2509.06730, 'Hyperbolic branching Brownian motion: \\ the empirical limit measure'] It would be interesting to study $\dim \Lambda$ and $\dim \mu_\infty$ in the presence of a drift: assume that there is a drift of strength $\lambda \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ away from the origin. If $\lambda>-1/2$, $\mu_\infty$ {should} still be supported on the boundary. For $\lambda <-1/2$, we {should} almost surely have that $\mu_\infty$ is a Dirac mass at the origin, and for $\lambda=-1/2$, $\mu_t$ {should} not converge. 

    If we were to consider drift away from a point $\zeta\in \partial\ensuremath{\mathbb{D}}$, the analysis becomes easy. By isometry, we can choose $\zeta =1${,} which corresponds to the unique boundary point $\infty$ at infinity in $\partial \ensuremath{\mathbb{H}}$. Geodesics going through $\infty$ in $\ensuremath{\mathbb{H}}$ are straight vertical lines. This means drift away from $\infty$ is a simple vertical drift of $\lambda$ (weighted by the hyperbolic metric). This means that {the} calculations in this work and in [lalley_hyperbolic_1997] still apply and we should get for $\lambda>-1/2$,
    \begin{equation*}
    \dim  \mu_\infty(\lambda) = [2\beta/(2\lambda+1)]\wedge 1,
    \end{equation*}
    and
    \begin{equation*}
        \dim \Lambda(\lambda) =
        \begin{cases}
            \frac{1}{2}\left(1+2\lambda - \sqrt{(1+2\lambda)^2-8\beta}\right) \ &\text{for }\beta \leq \frac{(1+2\lambda)^2}{8}, \\
            1 & \text{else}.
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}
    We believe the same expressions should still hold for drift away from the origin rather than from the boundary. The reason for this is that for $z\in \ensuremath{\mathbb{D}}$ we can replace drift away from the origin with drift away from $-\frac{z}{\vert z\vert}$. If we start a hyperbolic Brownian motion $Z_t$ at $z$ where $z$ is far away from the origin, then $-\frac{Z_t}{\vert Z_t\vert}$ should not vary much, hence we can replace drift away from the origin with drift away from a boundary point. (Stated as a question by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

[Lemma lem:Step3 of arXiv:2411.18775, 'Limit Theorems for the Dynamical Foundation of the Fractional Brownian Motion and Related '] Under assumptions of Theorem~(Thm of the paper)~(i), we have $\mathbb{P}$-almost surely 
		\begin{align*}
