[Definition 32 (Wiener isometry) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] For $k\in\N_0^N$, let $n = \abs{k}$. The map 
\begin{equation}

 I_n : e_k \longmapsto \frac{1}{\sqrt{n!}}\Phi_k
\end{equation} 
is called the $n$th \emph{Wiener isometry}.

[Definition signature of arXiv:2606.28869, '\bfseries A General Theory of Paths:\\ \large Signatures, Jump Lifts, and Expected Signatu'] Let $x:[0,T]\to E$ be a path of bounded variation. Its signature over $[s,t]$ is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Sig}(x)_{s,t}
=
\left(1,
\int_{s<u_1<t}\,\mathrm{d} x_{u_1},
\int_{s<u_1<u_2<t}\,\mathrm{d} x_{u_1}\otimes\,\mathrm{d} x_{u_2},
\ldots\right).
\end{equation}
For a word $w=i_1\cdots i_k$,
\begin{equation}
\inner{\mathrm{Sig}(x)_{s,t}}{w}
=
\int_{s<u_1<\cdots<u_k<t}\,\mathrm{d} x^{i_1}_{u_1}\cdots\,\mathrm{d} x^{i_k}_{u_k}.
\end{equation}

[Lemma prop:cardinality_increasing_dags of arXiv:2606.09386, 'High-degree vertices in uniform recursive directed acyclic graphs with freezing'] Fix a choice sequence $\mathbf x$ such that Assumption~$\hyperref[ass:A]{\mathbf{x}}$(item:geq1 of the paper) is satisfied. For $m,n\in\mathbb{N}$, we have
\begin{equation}
|\mathcal {I}_n^{(m)}(\mathbf x)| =  \prod_{\substack{2\leq i\leq n \\ x_i=1}} {A_{i-1} \choose m\wedge A_{i-1}} \prod_{\substack{2\leq i\leq n \\ x_i=-1}} A_{i-1},
\end{equation}
and the $\mathrm{URD}$ $G^{(n)}$ with choice sequence $\mathbf x$ is a uniform element of $\mathcal {I}_n^{(m)}(\mathbf x)$.
\proof
In the construction of an element of $\mathcal {I}_n^{(m)}(\mathbf x)$, for each step $i\in\mathcal{A}_n\setminus\{1\}$, there are $A_{i-1} \choose m\wedge A_{i-1}$ possible choices of active vertices to which the newly added vertex can be connected. Additionally, in each step $j\in\mathcal{F}_n$, we freeze exactly one of the $A_{j-1}$ active vertices. As the second label entry for frozen vertices is their freezing time (rather than the default value $a$ for active vertices), each frozen vertex's freezing time is uniquely identifiable in the final graph. Hence,
\begin{equation}
|\mathcal{I}_n^{(m)}(\mathbf x)|= \prod_{\substack{2\leq i\leq n \\ x_i=1}} {A_{i-1} \choose m\wedge A_{i-1}} \prod_{\substack{2\leq j\leq n \\ x_j=-1}} A_{j-1}. 
\end{equation}
Since for a URD, independently at each step $i$, if $x_i=1$, the choices of the distinct vertices are uniform among the $A_{i-1}$ active vertices, and if $x_i=-1$, a uniform random vertex among the $A_{i-1}$ active vertices is frozen, a URD is a uniform element of $\mathcal{I}_n^{(m)}(\mathbf x)$.\qed (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

