\end{equation}
If $s=r$, then the above equation holds but with the term $V_1<\min(V_{s+1},\cdots, V_r)$ deleted from the numerator and denominator.
We will assume from now on the $s<r$; the case $s=r$ follows similarly.
Let $Y=\min(V_3,\cdots V_s)$ and $Z=\min(V_{s+1},\cdots, V_r)$.
Then $V_1,V_2,Y,Z$ are independent and by Lemma (2 of the paper), $Y\sim\text{Exp}(\sum_{i=3}^s\theta_i)$ and ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:72abc9d149b903c68d46dd1f355e0c4073b6d20bfa95aab5141c8258a9521cb7","revision_digest":"sha256:eaa0123ea54276f00bbbab8aef206de678625056e85183ffecd0e39622d1443e","source_id":"arxiv:2605.10452","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

