	Note in particular that if $U_n$ is uniformly distributed on $\{1,\dots,n\}$ and independent of $\{\bm{Z}^n(k): 1 \leq k \leq n\}$, then $m_n$ is the law of $M_{U_n}^n$ as a random element in ${\mathcal C}$. By extending our probability space if necessary, we can assume that such random variables $U_n$ exist.

