[Lemma def:local of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] We define for any smooth function $f:\R^{2}\rightarrow\R$ 
\begin{equs}
   L^1(\R^2)\ni    \mathbf{L}_\tau f:(s,r)\mapsto \tau^{-1}f(s,s+(r-s)/\tau)\,.
\end{equs}
Then, setting $\mathbf{X}(s,r)\eqdef(s-r)$, the following equality holds true in a distributional sense:
\begin{equs}
f(s,r)=\delta (s-r) \int_\R f(s,w)\mathrm{d} w +\int_0^1\partial_{r}\text{$\mathbf{L}$}_\tau \big(\mathbf{X} f\big)(s,r)\mathrm{d}\tau\,.
\end{equs} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2608.30178, '2608.30178'] {
Assume that the model \eqref{eq:noimm-system} is source-conservative.  Fix \(M>1\) and
\(p\in(1,\alpha_1\wedge\alpha_2)\).  There exists a constant \(C_M>0\) such that,
for every almost surely finite stopping time \(T\) and every \(h\in(0,1]\),
\beqnn
 \E\left[
 \sup_{0\le u\le h}
 |X_{(T+u)\wedge\tau_M^+}-X_{T\wedge\tau_M^+}|^p
 \Bigm|\mathcal F_T\right]
 \le C_M(h^p+h^{p/2}+h).
 \eeqnn 
The same estimate holds with \(X\) replaced by \(Y\).
}

