[Theorem decomposition_summary of arXiv:2411.17653, 'Exclusion processes with non-reversible boundary: hydrodynamics and large deviations'] Fix a path $\pi(t,dx)=\rho_t(x)\;dx$ in $ {\mathcal{D}}([0,T],\mathscr{M}_{ac})$. Assume that $ {\rho}$ is continuous in $\Omega_T$, smooth in time and that there exists an $\epsilon>0$ such that $\epsilon\leq \rho_t(x)\leq 1-\epsilon,$  { for all } $(t,x)\in\Omega_T$ and that $I_{[0,T]}(\rho)<\infty$. Then,
    \begin{equation}
        I_{[0,T]}(\rho)=I^{(1)}_{[0,T]}(\rho)+I^{(2)}_{[0,T]}(\rho),
    \end{equation}
    for
    \begin{align}
        I^{(1)}_{[0,T]}(\rho)=\frac{1}{4}&\int_0^T\Big\{||M_t+\nabla u_t||^2_{\sigma(u_t)^{-1}}-R_t\Big\}\;dt,\\
        &I^{(2)}_{[0,T]}(\rho)=\int_0^T \Phi_t(a_t,b_t)\;dt,
    \end{align}
     {where $a_t$ and $b_t$ were introduced in (equation def_a_and_b of the paper) and $R_t$ was introduced at the end of Lemma (operators_with_M of the paper).} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

