{"artifact_digest":"sha256:6b76e9b9ed2dda82e0f87a1f2417901594d02a0d2f00998ac49e5592b4af8312","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/6b/6b76e9b9ed2dda82e0f87a1f2417901594d02a0d2f00998ac49e5592b4af8312.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:6b847409c543fec7d9cec98eb90305ebd5844c1e4f8a55f9be1ff68a44426255","bundle_entry_key":"2608-30638-theorem-14-staticity","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_129","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_129.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-30638-proposition-14-staticity","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Recall that we assume $g$ is pluriclosed, i.e. $dH=0$. It follows that
\begin{equation}
    \delta\theta=-*d*\theta=*dH=0.
\end{equation}
On the other hand, the soliton equation says that
\begin{equation}
 \delta H+\iota_{\nabla f}H=0.
\end{equation}
 By Lemma \ref{lem:four-dimensional-algebra} we have $\iota_XH=-*(\theta\wedge X^\flat)$ and therefore we get
     \(\delta H=*(\theta\wedge df),\)
 thus
\begin{equation}
      d\theta=df\wedge\theta.
\end{equation}

If the soliton is static, then \eqref{prop--Lie derivative of soliton vector field} shows that $V$
preserves both $J$ and $\omega$, and hence $g$.  Thus
