[Corollary 10 of arXiv:2607.08242, 'Convergence of random sums in non-commutative probability'] Suppose the assumptions of Theorem \ref{thm31}(ii) hold. 
    Suppose $\frac{N_n}{\eta_n}$ has a limiting probability measure $\mu$. Then, 
    \begin{equation*}
        \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\tilde{\varphi}(\tilde{\mathcal{S}}_{N_n}^m)=\varphi(s^m)\int_{0}^\infty x^{m/2}\,\mu(\mathrm{d}x),\ m\in\mathbb{N}.
        \end{equation*}
        In other words, $\tilde{\mathcal{S}}_{N_n}\stackrel{*}{\to}
        sy$ where $s$ and $y$ are independent variables, 

        and the $m$th moment of $y$ is given by $\int_0^\infty x^{m/2}\,\mu(\mathrm{d}x)$. If further $y$ has a probability measure $\mu_y$, and
        $\tilde{\mathcal{S}}_{N_n}$ has a probability measure $\mu_n$, then $\mu_n$ converges weakly to $\mu_s \star \mu_y$ where $\star$ denotes product convolution of two probability measures.

[Lemma 39 of arXiv:2607.08536, 'Soft edge limit of the Laguerre beta-ensemble at the lower edge'] Assume $a_n\ge 10/\beta+8$ and $2a_n/n\le \eps_n\le 1$. Then we have
    \begin{align}
        \ev\|(\mathsf{k}_{\beta,2a_n}^{(n)} -\tilde {\mathsf{k}}_{\beta,2a_n}^{(n)} )\ind(\max(x,y)\le 1-\eps_n) \|_2^2 \le \frac{10}{na_n}\log \eps_n^{-1}.
    \end{align}

