{"artifact_digest":"sha256:89b27c4bf6cf592b949211ebac74217b6fcd5a490c3e702a84530a51d7feddcc","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/89/89b27c4bf6cf592b949211ebac74217b6fcd5a490c3e702a84530a51d7feddcc.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:8870f545a8b8d0585d7756df7b450ca92e286e68e599d73963dbe5f5dd988095","bundle_entry_key":"2608-29316-theorem-4-theorem-4","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_120","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_120.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-29316-theorem-4-theorem-4","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Since $Z(2k+1)$ satisfies (\ref{1st}), it is clear that $A(2k+1)$ satisfies the intertwining relation (\ref{int-rel}) with $2r=2k+1$. Thus it is a conformally covariant differential operator of order $2k+1$. For the leading term of $A_{2k+1}$, we note that 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
&(-1)^k\a^0(\a^0\dc)^{k+1}(\dc\a^0)^k=(-1)^k\dc(\a^0\dc)^k(\dc\a^0)^k\\
&\quad =(-1)^k\cdot\dc^{2k+1}, \text{ since }\a^0\dc\text{ and }\dc\a^0 \text{ commute}.
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
Finally, 
